-- pending: #644, #745 (the kernel's `Div` at the surface: loops elaborate to `iterate`, fuel is a handler)
-- The kernel program `div-residual` (Backend/Kernel.lean, `Core/DivPrograms.lean`'s `divResidual`;
-- SPEC §14): a loop that never finishes on its own, whose state is a cell it adds one to at each
-- turn, run under fuel for 3 turns; the fuel closes it and hands the cell back, and the cell is
-- read and freed. Answer: i32[3]. The kernel program spells the loop with the kernel's `Div`
-- effect: its `tick`, `iterate` and the handler `div_fuel` (`divFuelV`), which neither the surface
-- nor the core text can write: the frontend elaborates loops to the library's codata `loop` and
-- its `div_fuel` until B2 (#644), and Backend2 has no lowering of `iterate` or the handler yet.
-- `corpus/kernel-div-residual.bc` is the same program in the core text over the library's loop.
-- Its surface form is #745's `with fuel(n) { … }`; the spelling below is a sketch of it.
-- Today `fuel` is no handler, and `with fuel(3) { … }` is read as a call given a closure.

fn spin(c: ref i32) -> ref i32 =
  let mut c = c;
  while true { let v = free(c); c = alloc(v + 1) };
  c

main -> i32 = let c = with fuel(3) { spin(alloc(0)) }; free(c)
